    101:| Möbius-side parity bisection of π(x) (paradigm-shift mode, no external technique) | PARTIAL | C | **B-grade refinement of E2.2.** Composes E2.2 (Liouville bisection π=(x−L)/2−C_3) with the standard Möbius rearrangement Q−M = 2·S_o, S_o = #{n≤x sqfree, ω odd}. Defines C_3*(x) := #{n≤x sqfree, composite, ω odd, ω≥3}. Identity verified integer-exact ∀x∈[1,10⁶]: **π(x)=(Q(x)−M(x))/2−C_3*(x)** (F1) and **NS_o(x)=(x−Q−L+M)/2** with NS_o:=#{not sqfree, Ω odd} (F2, bridge identity); one-line proof Σμ²λ=Σμ=M. Consequence: **C_3(x)−C_3*(x)=NS_o(x)** (F3). 4-cell decomposition (squarefree × Ω-parity) closed-form: S_e=(Q+M)/2, S_o=(Q−M)/2, NS_e=((x−Q)+(L−M))/2, NS_o=(x−Q−L+M)/2. Empirical Möbius-side parity bisection inherits E1.6's near-independence: I(B(x) mod 2 ; C_3*(x) mod 2) ≈ 1.15·10⁻⁵ bits at N=10⁶, same order as I(A;C_3) ≈ 2·10⁻⁵. Falsifiers F1–F6 ALL PASS (zero failures dict at N=10⁶). **Why C**: (Q−M)/2 ~ 0.304·x dominated by C_3* ~ 0.304·x with π~x/log x as needle; computing C_3*(x) to π(x)-precision reduces to counting squarefree k-almost-primes which itself reduces to π in arithmetic progressions (E3.4 obstruction). No polylog opening. Algorithmic: same x^{2/3} via M(x). **What's new**: the Möbius bisection itself, the bridge identity (one-line proof), the 4-cell decomposition, and the empirical Möbius-side parity-independence — none of these were recorded in EDGES.md or CLOSED_PATHS.md before. NO external technique imported (paradigm-shift discipline). Composes: E2.2, E1.6, E2.10, folklore Möbius `Q−M=2·S_o`. See `experiments/constructions/mobius_bisection_of_pi/`, `archive/sessions/session427_mobius_bisection.md`. | 427 |
    336:| §D.D41 ATTACK_VECTORS — Gurau-Witten melonic universality on the chi_P 3-tensor `T_{ijk} = 1[i+j+k=N] chi_P(i)chi_P(j)chi_P(k)` (cross-domain: random tensor models / 1/N expansion / SYK without disorder; FIRST melonic / random-tensor import in project, CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES §3 promoted PROPOSED → USED-E) | FAIL | E | **WILD-SWING SESSION attempting A-grade frontier target.** D41 asks whether the chi_P 3-tensor lies in the Gurau-Witten melonic universality class — orthogonal to all 35+ rank-2 pseudorandomness measurements on chi_P. **Mode-1 unfolding theorem (S424 first formulation):** `M_1 M_1^T` is provably DIAGONAL because the (j, k)-supports of different rows i are disjoint antidiagonals (i+j+k=N constraint). Mode-1 spectrum = trivially the multiset {sqrt(r_2(N-i)) : chi_P(i)=1} — Goldbach 2-rep on antidiagonals. **Constraint-eliminated matricisation `M_2[i,j] = chi_P(i)chi_P(j)chi_P(N-i-j)`** (the natural rank-2 reduction): symmetric (N-9) x (N-9) matrix, nontrivial. **Phase 2 (density-matched Bernoulli null, B=30 reals, N ∈ {1023, 2047}):** chi_P sigma_max 4.5-13.2 sigma above null; chi_P rho_top = sigma_max^2/F^2 7.1-25.9 sigma above null. **Phase 3 (F^2-matched Bernoulli null at calibrated q*, A-vs-B critical test, N ∈ {1023, 2047, 4095, 8191, 16383}):** sigma_max excess COLLAPSES to 0.7-7.4 sigma after HL-S_3(N) ternary-count adjustment; rho_top excess persists at 4.8-33.8 sigma, peaking at Mersenne prime N=8191 (factorisation-correlated). **Phase 4 (DEFINITIVE — leading eigenvector vs HL R_2(N-p)):** at N=2047 and N=8191, Spearman(empirical degree d_p, HL Goldbach R_2(N-p)) = 0.993-0.997; Cosine(v_1, sqrt(HL_R_2(N-p))) = 0.988; Spearman(v_1^2, empirical d_p) = 0.985-0.990. **Conclusion: chi_P 3-tensor matricisation is rank-1 Perron-Frobenius with leading eigenvector v_p ∝ sqrt(R_2(N-p)) ∝ sqrt(HL Goldbach prediction).** The Phase 3 rho_top residual is fully explained by the HL VARIANCE prediction for R_2(N-p) over primes (Bernoulli at F^2-matched q* has Poisson-variance d_p, chi_P has HL-correlated d_p). **Melonic universality REJECTED** — the Vinogradov i+j+k=N constraint reduces the 3-tensor to a 2-tensor (Goldbach plane); the "i.i.d. random tensor" required for melonic class is structurally absent because chi_P(i)chi_P(j)chi_P(k) factorises. **41st pseudorandomness measure landing at HL noise floor**, in the new "rank-3 universality class" category. **At N=16383 = 3·43·127 (smooth):** |E_2|/|E_1| = 0.97 — near-degenerate top eigenvalues; v_1 mixes two PF-type modes, dropping cosine to 0.81; emp_d still tracks HL at 0.996 (degree distribution unaffected by eigenvector mixing). Refines E2.13 by an explicit rank-3 spectral identity, but no new edge promoted (honest-failure clause: would-be edge "rank-1 PF + HL singular series" is just a paraphrase of E2.13 + classical Vinogradov circle-method result). **Cross-domain refs:** Gurau 2011 *Comm. Math. Phys.* 304, 69 = arXiv:1011.2726; Witten 2016 arXiv:1610.09758; Klebanov-Tarnopolsky 2017 *Phys. Rev. D* 95, 046004 = arXiv:1611.08915; Bonzom-Gurau-Riello-Rivasseau 2011 *Nucl. Phys. B* 853, 174 = arXiv:1105.3122. **Cites** E2.13, E3.1, E7.1 (orthogonal rank-2). See `experiments/constructions/d41_chip_3tensor/{d41_chip_3tensor.py, d41_eigvec_analysis.py, d41_f2_matched.py, d41_eigvec_hl.py, d41_chip_3tensor_results.md}`, `archive/sessions/session424_d41_gurau_witten_chip_3tensor.md`. **Self-grade B (ambitious frontier-attack failure, structural).** | 424 |
    887:| §F6 / §P7 — does the dyadic family π(2^k), k = 1..N, admit per-query polylog amortisation beyond generic-magnitude HKM? (NOVELTY_CHALLENGES F6 / OPEN_POSITIVE_TARGETS P7 per-query side) | EXPERIMENT (B-NEGATIVE: three independent structural tests on (π(2^k))_{k=1..56} all return random-baseline values within Monte Carlo noise) | E (E1.1 + E1.5; refines E1.1 inline) | S246 novelty mode. Built `experiments/wildcard/dyadic_pi_structure/dyadic_pi_structure.py`: (1) hard-coded OEIS A007053 reference values π(2^k) for k=0..56, verified k≤18 against sympy.primepi (18/18 exact); (2) computed Li(2^k) and Riemann R(2^k) at 80-decimal mpmath precision, residual r_R(k) = π(2^k) − R(2^k), normalised c_R(k) = r_R(k)/2^{k/2}; (3) Monte Carlo random-binary BM linear-complexity null at length 56 with matched n_pos=25 / n_neg=31 split (4000 shuffles): mean=28.25, std=1.01, p05=27, p95=30; data BM(sign(r_R))=28, sits at 0.25σ below MC mean — F1 (BM ≤ MC p05) FAILS; (4) MC iid-Gaussian lag-1 |autocorr| null (4000 trials): p95=0.250, p99=0.328, p999=0.437; data max |ac| over lags 1..10 = 0.283 at lag 1, below MC p99, Bonferroni-corrected over 10 lags p ≈ 0.4 — F2 (max |ac| ≥ MC p999) FAILS; (5) HKM cost test via fresh subprocess sympy.primepi with no sieve-cache reuse: median T(2^k) / mean(T(2^k − 1), T(2^k + 1)) = 0.998 / 1.047 / 0.995 at k = 28, 30, 32 (avg 1.013) — F3 (dyadic speedup ratio ≤ 0.5) FAILS. **All three F-claims FAIL → B-NEGATIVE per-query closure.** Note: an earlier v1 result with Li-baseline gave spurious lag-1 = 0.87 driven by the smooth -Li(√x)/2 Möbius-series correction; switching to R-baseline (which absorbs the smooth Σ μ(n)/n Li(x^{1/n}) series) eliminates the confound. **Structural reason** (two independent mechanisms): (a) information-theoretic — the explicit-formula zero-sum −Σ_ρ R(x^ρ) ≈ −2√x Σ_n cos(γ_n log x − arg(ρ_n))/|ρ_n| has phases γ_n · k log 2 mod 2π Weyl-equidistributed in k for every γ_n with γ_n irrational, independently of the dyadic form; (b) algorithmic — Lucy / Meissel-Lehmer outer loop processes {n ≤ √x} and x^{2/3}-smooth counts which carry no binary-representation side-channel. **What this rules out**: (i) any algorithm computing π(2^k) substantially faster than generic-x π(x) at the same magnitude; (ii) any compressed representation of (π(2^k))_k of size below the trivial Σ_k k = O(N²) output bit-budget; (iii) any short-recurrence/closed-form for residuals r_R(k) at the n=56 scale. **What this does NOT close**: cross-k batched amortisation (Thread-5 / Correlation-Dichotomy shape transposed to dyadic family) — the shared zero database (γ_n)_{n=1..K} is k-independent and admits per-query O(K) cost with K = polylog. Registered as F6.b in NOVELTY_CHALLENGES.md and P7.b in OPEN_POSITIVE_TARGETS.md. **Successor challenges proposed in S246**: F6.a (cross-modulus generalisation x_k = m^k for m ∈ {3, 5, 6, 10, 30}), F6.b (batched-on-k amortisation), F6.c (rigorous Ω(N²) lower bound from output bit-budget). **Cross-domain ingredient**: Berlekamp-Massey linear complexity over GF(2) (signal-processing standard). CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md addition: "Berlekamp-Massey + Monte Carlo random-shuffle null for testing low-complexity linear recurrences in arithmetic sequences — USED-E (S246), confirmed n=56 too short to discriminate polylog from random; minimum useful length n > log²(n) · 5 ≈ 200". **Cites** E1.1 (per-query HKM x^{2/3} bound; refined inline with dyadic per-query no-amortisation note), E1.5 (per-step 0.537 entropy; consistent with Weyl-equidistribution mechanism). See `experiments/wildcard/dyadic_pi_structure/{dyadic_pi_structure.py, dyadic_pi_structure_results.md, raw_data.json, hkm_cost.json, summary.json}`, `archive/sessions/session246_f6_dyadic_pi_structure.md`. | 246 |
    891:| §RE-VERIFY E2.28 — Baker-Norine q-reduced form of D_P^N: was the S161 closure conservative? Specifically, does the divisor `D_Ω₁(n) = 1[Ω(n)=1]` (chips at primes AND prime powers `p^k`, `k ≥ 2`) — NOT tested in S161 — admit a q-reduction identity beyond S161's `{1} ∪ {primes}` support hypothesis? | REFINEMENT (B-grade case (i): two new identities S425.1 and S425.2 extend E2.28's scope; sharpened closure mechanism articulated as graph-topological depth-1 / leaf collection) | E (refines E2.28 inline) | S425 re-verify-closure adversarial probe of E2.28 (S161). **Probe**: `s425_inverse_chipfiring_probe.py` runs q-reduction on six divisors {D_P, D_uniform, D_sink_K=N, D_τ, D_μ², D_Ω₁} × {Γ_N, H_N} × N ∈ {16, 32, 64, 128} (48 runs). Companion `s425_verify_omega1_identity.py` extends to N=256 and prints full chip distribution. **Identity S425.1 (new)**: on H_N with sink q=1, q-reduce(D_Ω₁)·(1) = π(N) exactly. Σ_{v≠1} D'_Ω₁(v) = π(√N) + π(N^{1/3}) + π(N^{1/4}) + ⋯ = #{prime powers p^k : k ≥ 2, p^k ≤ N}. Verified at N ∈ {16, 32, 64, 128, 256} (5/5). Off-sink chips are scattered (NOT confined to prime powers themselves) but their TOTAL is the prime-power-k≥2 count by chip conservation. **Identity S425.2 (new)**: on Γ_N with sink q=1, q-reduce(D_Ω₁)·(1) = π(N) − π(N/2), identical to D_P. Verified at N ∈ {16, 32, 64, 128} (4/4). **Sharpened closure mechanism**: S161 said "building D_P^N requires knowing primes" (input-divisor-level closure). S425 sharpens to extraction-mechanism-level: on H_N, D'(1) reads off the *prime-restricted chip total* via depth-1 collection (q's depth-1 neighbours in H_N are exactly primes ≤ N; chips at depth ≥ 2 are graph-topologically blocked from single-step lending to q and contribute 0 to sink). On Γ_N, sink reads #{leaves of Γ_N with chips} = #{primes p : N/2 < p ≤ N}. **Decomposition lemma**: D_Ω₁ = D_P + D_higher (higher prime powers). q-reduce(D_higher)(1) = 0 because chips at p^k, k ≥ 2, are not adjacent to q in H_N. So q-reduce(D_Ω₁)(1) = q-reduce(D_P)(1) + q-reduce(D_higher)(1) = π(N) + 0 = π(N). **Why closure persists**: identifying H_N's depth-1 neighbours of q is polylog-equivalent to enumerating primes ≤ N; identifying Γ_N's leaves is polylog-equivalent to enumerating primes in (N/2, N]. Graph construction itself costs Ω(N) and includes prime detection. **What this rules out**: (i) D_Ω₁ as an algorithmic-novelty input that bypasses S161's "input requires primes" — BUILDING D_Ω₁ across [1, N] still costs Ω(N polylog) for prime-power testing; (ii) the hypothesis that S161's identity-class is restricted to {1} ∪ {primes} support — refuted by D_Ω₁; (iii) the hypothesis that chips at higher prime powers contribute non-zero to sink under q-reduction on H_N — directly tested, contributes exactly 0. **What this does NOT close**: (i) alternative reduction mechanisms (multi-sink reduction, weighted chip-firing) — outside slot scope; (ii) divisors supported on graph-topologically distinguished sets that DON'T coincide with primes — none currently known. **Verdict**: B-grade case (i) — refinement of E2.28 with precise new statements (S425.1 and S425.2) extending the scope of S161's generalised identity, plus sharpened closure mechanism (depth-1 / leaf collection). Closure stands. **Cites** E2.28 (refined inline), E2.29 (graph-topological closure analogue cross-reference), S161 (parent closure). Cross-domain technique: chip-firing on arithmetic graphs (Baker-Norine 2007 + Dhar 1990) — already USED-I via E2.28; this slot REFINES the scope without importing new technique. See `experiments/algebraic/baker_norine_chi_p/{s425_inverse_chipfiring_probe.py, s425_verify_omega1_identity.py, s425_inverse_chipfiring_results.md, s425_inverse_chipfiring_results.json}`, `archive/sessions/session425_reverify_e2_28.md`. | 425 |
    892:| §F6.a — does the dyadic per-query no-amortisation closure (S246, B-NEGATIVE) generalise to other parametric families x_k = m^k for m > 2? (NOVELTY_CHALLENGES F6.a successor of S246) | EXPERIMENT (B-NEGATIVE-universal: F1 BM-compressibility AND F2 max-lag-autocorr above MC p999 FAIL for every m ∈ {3, 5, 6, 10, 30}) | E (E1.1 + E1.5; refines E1.1 inline) | S426 novelty mode. Built `experiments/wildcard/cross_modulus_pi_structure/` + ran on m ∈ {3, 5, 6, 10, 30} with K-budgets {22, 15, 14, 28, 8} (m=10 uses OEIS A006880 verified vs sympy.primepi for k ≤ 9; other m via sympy.primepi at k_max ≤ 30^8 = 6.6e11). Three structural tests on residual `c_R(k) = (π(m^k) − R(m^k)) / m^{k/2}`: (i) BM linear complexity of sign(r_R(k)) over GF(2) vs MC random-shuffle null; (ii) max |autocorr(c_R, lag ℓ)| over `1 ≤ ℓ ≤ min(10, K_m/3)` vs MC iid-Gaussian null. Per-m verdicts (BM/MC.p05, max\|ac\|/MC.p999): m=3 (11/10, 0.319/0.616); m=5 (9/6, 0.343/0.717); m=6 (8/6, 0.529/0.687); m=10 (14/13, 0.342/0.574); m=30 (5/3, 0.336/0.832). All inside MC null bands; F1 + F2 fail 5/5. Aggregate verdict B_NEGATIVE_universal_cross_modulus — Weyl equidistribution of `(γ_n · k log m mod 2π)` (`log m` transcendental for every integer m ≥ 2 by Lindemann-Weierstrass) forces the same null-baseline shape across moduli. Bonus γ_1-cosine ceiling: `\|emp_lag1_ac(m)\| ≤ \|cos(γ_1·log(m) mod 2π)\| + 0.05` saturates 5/5 cross-modulus cases (and 6/6 retroactively including m=2); m=6 identified as near-resonance (`φ_1(6) = 0.193 rad`, ceiling +0.981, empirical lag-1 = 0.529 = 54% of ceiling). Sign agreement of `cos(φ_1)` vs `emp_lag1`: 3/5 m. Refines E1.1 inline. Successor challenges proposed: §F6.a.i (γ_1 ceiling tightness scan over m ∈ {2..50}), §F6.a.ii (γ_2 decoherence rate as K_m → ∞), §F6.a.iii (higher-lag ceiling breakdown). Files: `experiments/wildcard/cross_modulus_pi_structure/{cross_modulus_pi_structure.py, cross_modulus_pi_structure_results.md, gamma1_phase_diagnostic.py, gamma1_phase_diagnostic.json, raw_data.json, summary.json}`, `archive/sessions/session426_f6a_cross_modulus.md`. | 426 |
